EVERY SHORT UNCONDITIONAL BRANCH HALFWORD.

English Major C: precise source-level contract for one COMPLETE
ARMv7-A Thumb instruction-decoding region.
Status: architectural description, complete finite encoding inventory,
and independent temporary assembler/decoder checks. NOT a complete
ARMv7-A T32 ISA, a verified compiler, an interpreter, or execution
on an ARM CPU.

SCOPE AND SOURCE OF AUTHORITY.

Exactly every sixteen-bit halfword with numerical value from
hexadecimal E000 through hexadecimal E7FF, inclusive, belongs to
the short, immediate, T32 B T2 instruction encoding family.
No other halfword belongs to this finite region.

The normative reference is ARM DDI 0406C.d, publication ID040418,
Chapter A6.2's sixteen-bit Thumb top-level decoder and
section A8.8.18 "B", encoding T2 (page A8-332).
Official source:
https://documentation-service.arm.com/static/5f8daeb7f86e16515cdb8c4e

The complete 2048-row enumeration belongs at
  _/fixtures/armv7-a-t32-unconditional-branch-halfwords.tsv
relative to the repository root. Its data rows are NOT ARM
instructions executed by merely reading a TSV file.
They encode a claim that can be checked independently.

This instruction family maps to EXISTING English Major C
operation number 42, whose long English name begins:
"Replace the execution index with the named legal T32
instruction address without modifying the link register."

This work does not admit a NEW operation and does not
change the current 72-operation closed permission grant.
It narrows the verified encoding forms inside operation 42.

PART ONE. WHAT THE 2048 DIFFERENT BIT PATTERNS MEAN.

A T32 T2 short immediate B has exactly sixteen bits.
The five most significant bits, bits 15 through 11,
are, in order, one, one, one, zero, zero.
In binary, this fixed prefix is 11100.
The other eleven bits, numbered 10 through 0,
encode an immediate quantity called imm11.

Every possible sequence of eleven bits occurs:
  00000000000 through 11111111111.
There are 2^11, or precisely 2048, possible imm11
values, and all are accounted for by the finite table.

The numerical halfword value is 0xE000 + imm11.
The first halfword of this instruction does NOT
begin with a T32 prefix 11101, 11110 or 11111.
It is a COMPLETE TWO-BYTE instruction. No second
halfword is consumed as part of this encoding.

The prefix and the imm11 field contain NO general-purpose
register number, NO LR destination, NO comparison result,
NO flag-changing option, and NO operand indicating that
a linker should automatically make this a subroutine call.

The instruction's PC-relative displacement is a
TWO'S-COMPLEMENT signed twelve-bit number obtained
from the eleven-bit imm11 followed by one binary zero.
It is not the unsigned numerical value imm11 multiplied
by two for every input.

Define the signed mathematical integer value of imm11:
  If imm11 is less than 1024, signed_imm11 = imm11.
  If imm11 is 1024 or greater, signed_imm11 = imm11 - 2048.

Define the BYTE displacement:
  branch_displacement_bytes = 2 * signed_imm11.

That displacement is an even integer between
negative 2048 and positive 2046, inclusive.
For every possible value, the target can be computed
from the exact instruction's address and architectural
PC operand. This describes encoder reach relative
to the PC base, not a promise that the target page
is mapped or that an intended instruction begins there.

PART TWO. THE EXECUTION INDEX AND PROGRAM-COUNTER OPERAND.

Let the current T32 instruction begin at virtual byte
address A, which must identify a halfword-aligned
instruction boundary in the checked English program.

The architectural PC-operand base for this encoding is A+4,
NOT A+2, even though the current instruction is only TWO
bytes long.

The branch target is:
  TARGET = (A + 4 + branch_displacement_bytes)
           subject to the architecture's address-width
           and valid-target requirements.

For ordinary execution OUTSIDE an IT block, a valid
B T2 is taken without using the current N/Z/C/V flags.
The execution index becomes TARGET. It does not
first execute the halfword at A+2 and THEN branch.

It preserves R0 through R12, SP, LR, and the
arithmetic condition flags N, Z, C, V.
The instruction does not load from data memory,
write data memory, save LR, or automatically
change the instruction set to A32.

The next instruction is fetched from the target
ONLY if the applicable execution state and memory
permissions make that fetch legal. Branching to
unmapped or non-executable memory is not permission
to run the bytes stored there.

A source label represents a promised instruction
entry point. The encoded signed displacement is
the actual native restriction. If a source label
cannot be reached by the chosen encoding, the
compiler/linker must either choose a separately
permitted sequence/encoding or reject the lowering.
A veneer introduced by a linker is itself actual
machine code and requires its own retained bytes
and English translation.

PART THREE. IMPORTANT NUMERICAL BOUNDARIES.

Take A=0x00001000 as an illustrative instruction address.

Hexadecimal E000 has imm11 equal to zero,
therefore displacement zero, and target 0x00001004.
A zero displacement does NOT mean next sequential
address 0x00001002.

Hexadecimal E001 has imm11 equal to one,
therefore displacement positive two, target 0x00001006.

Hexadecimal E3FF has imm11=1023, the largest
nonnegative signed eleven-bit integer,
therefore displacement positive 2046,
and target 0x00001802.

Hexadecimal E400 has imm11=1024, interpreted
as negative 1024 after sign extension,
therefore displacement negative 2048,
and target 0x00000804.

Hexadecimal E7FD has imm11=2045, interpreted
as signed negative three, displacement
negative six, and target 0x00000FFE.

Hexadecimal E7FE has imm11=2046, interpreted
as negative two, displacement negative four,
and target A itself, 0x00001000. Under
ordinary successful execution it branches
back to the SAME machine instruction.
An interrupt, exception or later external
observer still requires its own architecture
and environment rules; do not infer absolute
physical nontermination merely from this encoding.

Hexadecimal E7FF has imm11=2047, signed
negative one, displacement negative two.
Its TARGET is 0x00001002, numerically identical
to the usual sequential successor for a
two-byte instruction. Outside IT, the branch
is nevertheless TAKEN; the matching address
is not evidence that no branch operation
was executed.

The table's target addresses are calculated
for the assumed A=0x1000, not measured physical
processor addresses. They are illustrative
virtual-byte-address arithmetic.

PART FOUR. THE WORD "UNCONDITIONAL" NEEDS ITS PREMISES.

The T2 encoding has no condition-code field.
But ARM DDI 0406C.d A8.8.18 explicitly permits
B T2 OUTSIDE or LAST INSIDE an IT block.

OUTSIDE an IT block:
  The branch's normal effect is unconditional.
  Its target is the architectural next instruction.

INSIDE an IT block, as the LAST slot:
  The ITSTATE supplies the governing condition.
  If that condition holds, the branch effect occurs
  and the execution index becomes its encoded target.
  If the IT condition fails, the branch's normal
  control-transfer effect is suppressed. The processor
  advances past the TWO-BYTE B T2 instruction and
  updates ITSTATE in the architecture-defined way.
  Both the condition and the width remain observable
  parts of the faithful English translation.

INSIDE an IT block, but NOT in its last slot:
  The exact B T2 form is architecturally UNPREDICTABLE.
  The checked source profile must REJECT it.
  Merely calling B an "unconditional branch" must not
  excuse its placement early in IT.

The different T1 conditional branch in D000..DDFF
is not permitted in IT at all, even in the final
slot. The same assembler-like words B or BEQ
do not confer identical native encoding rights.

An independent LLVM disassembly diagnostic produced:
  0xE000 outside IT: printed as "b" with offset zero;
  0xE000 as the one and last slot of IT EQ:
      printed as "beq" with offset zero.

This is NOT a mutation of instruction bytes.
The B T2 encoding 0xE000 has not changed.
The disassembler uses preceding ITSTATE to
display the EFFECTIVE condition of the same bytes.
A source decoder that inspects 0xE000 alone must
state "B T2 encoded without its own condition field";
a full execution trace must additionally state
whether it is currently governed by ITSTATE.

PART FIVE. A COMPLETE ENCODING PARTITION.

Each integer imm11 from 0 through 2047 appears
EXACTLY ONCE in the committed TSV, in ascending order.
No two distinct imm11 values produce the same raw
sixteen-bit halfword in E000..E7FF.

The partition by imm11 sign is:
  E000..E3FF: 1024 nonnegative imm11 interpretations.
  E400..E7FF: 1024 negative imm11 interpretations.
Together the two sets contain 2048 encodings.

All 2048 are allocated to the SAME architectural
B T2 instruction form, but a complete successful
program still needs a legal IT position,
a valid executable instruction target and the
specified processor/environment state.

The table therefore makes two distinct claims:
  1. Exactly these 2048 raw halfwords are allocated
     to this native decoding class.
  2. The current English profile grants operation 42
     for a particular halfword only when its actual
     execution context, destination and other
     architecture restrictions are satisfied.

It would be false to translate "all 2048 are allocated"
as "all 2048 always execute correctly in every program."

PART SIX. RETAIN EVERY IMPORTANT ALTERNATIVE.

Alternative A. Use a narrow two-byte B T2 branch.
Benefit: two instruction bytes and a simple
signed PC-relative displacement.
Cost: limited displacement reach; a branch
can disrupt sequential instruction fetching.

Alternative B. Use a four-byte T32 B T4 form
where supported and permitted by an extended exact
encoding contract. Benefit: much longer reach.
Cost: longer instruction, different immediate
bit assembly and branch encoding restrictions.
The T2 permission does NOT authorize T4.

Alternative C. Use the T1 sixteen-bit conditional
B after a compare. Benefit: makes the predicate
explicit within the branch encoding itself.
Cost: signed displacement only -256..+254
bytes, no placement in IT, and different
flag-dependent execution semantics.

Alternative D. Use B T2 as the last instruction
inside a legal IT block. Benefit: conditional
branch effect without a condition-coded branch
halfword. Cost: IT state and a preceding IT
instruction; cannot be used in an earlier slot.

Alternative E. Invert a condition and alter
fall-through layout so the ordinary next instruction
executes in the frequently observed case.
Benefit: possible instruction locality.
Cost: exact source/target relocation and a
performance result dependent on the actual core.

Alternative F. Use register-indirect BX with
a value representing an instruction target.
Benefit: indirect control transfer and state
selection. Cost: source register state,
Thumb-state tag and security/entry constraints.
Not equivalent to immediate B and cannot
be silently introduced by a friendly name.

Alternative G. Use table branches TBB/TBH
for bounded multiway dispatch.
Benefit: a table of DATA can determine a branch target.
Cost: extra memory read, bounds and target integrity
obligations. Not a direct B encoding.

Alternative H. Allow a linker to introduce
a veneer for an out-of-range target.
Benefit: can preserve programmer intent
despite local branch range.
Cost: additional actual machine instructions;
their bytes and effects must be disclosed,
not omitted from bottom-up translation.

Alternative I. Rely on physical prediction,
prefetch, cache or speculation to hide
a branch's cost. Possible advantage depends
on the actual CPU. No architectural
instruction-count argument establishes
performance or speculative side effects.
The architectural next index remains the
branch-selected target under the declared
execution model.

Alternative J. Use no branch because the
numerical B T2 target equals sequential A+2
(E7FF) and replace it with NOP.
The resulting architectural next ADDRESS may
match outside IT, but the two are not
necessarily identical in control-flow,
exception behavior, IT context, or performance.
This is a proposed optimization requiring
a complete observation/equivalence contract,
not an assumed identity of instructions.

No candidate is accepted as universally
faster based solely on these source descriptions.

PART SEVEN. SOURCE AND DECODER EVIDENCE.

A diagnostic written and executed on a Linux
x86-64 host used clang 17.0.0, target
armv7a-none-eabi with Thumb enabled, and
llvm-objdump 17.0.0 with Thumb triple.

Its assembly source used:
    .syntax unified
    .arch armv7-a
    .thumb
    .text
    .thumb_func
    all_e:
and literal .hword directives listing
every halfword E000..E7FF in increasing order.

The resulting ELF32 little-endian ARM object
contained those bytes in its text section.
LLVM objdump decoded each as one two-byte
B instruction. The diagnostic checked every
printed halfword, byte address, signed
immediate displacement and target against
the independent bitfield formula above.

There were 2048 decoder matches and ZERO
unexplained mismatches. Those were not
2048 ARM CPU executions; this was
a foreign disassembler comparison.

The associated evidence receipt documents
the source/object/disassembly SHA256 digests,
host tool versions and exact check boundaries.
These source files are not proof that
a first-party English Major C parser or
semantic interpreter exists.

PART EIGHT. ACCEPTANCE CASES THAT MUST BE MECHANICALLY REJECTED.

1. Interpret an E400 imm11 as positive 1024
   and branch FORWARD by 2048 bytes.
2. Use current instruction address A rather than
   PC operand A+4 as the displacement base.
3. Interpret E000's zero immediate as sequential A+2.
4. Treat E7FE as a branch to A+2 rather than A.
5. Change LR when executing immediate B T2.
6. Change N/Z/C/V when B T2 executes or is skipped.
7. Treat E000 as a four-byte instruction.
8. Decode the next halfword as part of E000.
9. Execute B T2 when its enclosing IT condition is false.
10. Allow a B T2 in a nonfinal IT slot.
11. Forbid every B T2 in IT solely because
    the distinct T1 conditional B is forbidden there.
12. Treat E000 rendered "beq" by LLVM under IT
    as if its raw bytes had become a D0xx encoding.
13. Claim the target of an E7FF instruction is NOT
    equal to A+2; conversely, claim that matching
    numerical address proves no branch occurred.
14. Declare an unmapped or non-executable target valid
    because the immediate offset can represent it.
15. Claim an out-of-range branch was directly encoded
    in a narrow T2 halfword without a distinct
    authorized expansion or linker instruction.
16. Treat the 2048 classified forms as
    the complete ARMv7-A T32 instruction set.

The first classification/displacement checks have
temporary diagnostic evidence. The complete
execution-context, exception and target-memory
cases remain pending independent AICI acceptance.

END OF E000..E7FF COMPLETE DECODE REGION.
